# React security

Stack-specific risks for React component files (`*.tsx`/`*.jsx`, W1-AC8),
narrowing `ts-js-node`'s generic security rule. Not a general OWASP list —
only the sinks React's rendering model makes reachable.

## `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`

- Treat `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` as the default XSS sink: any user-, API-,
  or CMS-sourced string passed to it renders as live HTML/JS. Avoid it —
  render the content as text/JSX instead — unless the project genuinely
  needs to render rich HTML (e.g. a CMS body).
- When it is unavoidable, sanitize the string through the project's own
  sanitizer (e.g. DOMPurify) immediately before the prop is built, not
  earlier in the pipeline where a later edit could drop the sanitize step;
  never pass a raw string straight through.
- Never build the `__html` value by concatenating user input into a
  template string of markup — that is the same sink with extra steps.

## URLs from user data

- Reject or scheme-allowlist a URL that becomes an `href`/`src` when it
  comes from user input, a query param, or an API response —
  `javascript:`, `data:`, and `vbscript:` schemes execute on click/load.
  Validate against an allowlist (`http:`, `https:`, `mailto:`) before
  binding it to the element.
- The same applies to a dynamic `src` on `<img>`, `<iframe>`, or `<script>`
  built from anything not fully controlled by the app.

## Secrets and client bundles

- Anything read via the framework's public-env convention (e.g. a
  `NEXT_PUBLIC_`/`VITE_`/`REACT_APP_`-prefixed variable, or any value the
  bundler inlines into client output) ships in the browser bundle in plain
  text — never put an API secret, private key, or unscoped token behind
  one of these prefixes. A value that must stay server-only belongs in a
  server component, a route handler/API route, or a server function/
  action, never a client component or a plain (non-prefixed-and-still-
  bundled) constant imported into client code.
- Grep a diff for the project's public-env prefix before approving a new
  environment variable used inside a client component.
- A Server Function/Action is not merely a safe home for a server-only
  value — calling it from the client turns it into a public, directly
  reachable HTTP endpoint (its own route, invocable by anything that can
  reach the app, not only through the form/button that references it in
  the source). It gets none of the caller-identity or authorization
  checks a UI-only flow implies; each Server Function/Action must
  authenticate the caller, authorize the specific operation, and validate
  every argument itself, exactly as a hand-written API route would,
  before it touches data or a mutation.

## Third-party scripts and client-side auth

- Loading a third-party script by injecting a `<script>` tag with a
  dynamic or user-influenced `src` is the same class of risk as an
  unvalidated `href`/`src` above; pin third-party script URLs to a fixed,
  reviewed list.
- Do not gate a protected view purely on client-side state (a store flag,
  a decoded-but-unverified JWT, a route guard component) — a client
  component can always be bypassed by disabling JavaScript or calling the
  underlying API directly. Client-side checks are UX only; the real
  authorization check must happen server-side (server component, route
  handler, or backend).
